I found the likely test failure in `app.js`: the Express server was started unconditionally when the module was imported.

That often breaks tests because test runners import `app` and manage the server lifecycle themselves; calling `app.listen(3000)` during import can leave open handles or cause port conflicts.

Changed:

```js
app.listen(3000, () => {
  console.log('Server running on port 3000');
});
```

to:

```js
if (require.main === module) {
  app.listen(3000, () => {
    console.log('Server running on port 3000');
  });
}
```

Now `app.js` only starts the server when run directly with `node app.js`, while tests can safely `require('./app')`.

